==English==

===Etymology===
{{unc|en}}, but likely of {{bor|en|gmq|-}} origin, akin to {{cog|da|dingle}}, {{m|da|dangle}}, {{cog|sv|dangla||to swing about}}, {{cog|no|dangla}}, perhaps via {{der|en|frr|dangeln}};<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> all possibly related to {{der|en|non|dengja|t=to hit}}.<ref>{{R:SAOB online|dangla}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdæŋ.ɡəl/|[ˈdæŋ.ɡl̩]|a=non-æ-tensing}}
** {{audio|en|En-au-dangle.ogg|a=AU}}
*** {{IPA|en|/ˈdeɪ̯ŋ.ɡəl/|[ˈdeɪ̯ŋ.ɡl̩]|/ˈdɛ̃ŋ.ɡəl/|[ˈdɛ̃ŋ.ɡl̩]|a=æ-tensing}}
* {{rhymes|en|æŋɡəl|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|dan|gle}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[hang]] [[loosely]] with the ability to [[swing]].
#: {{ux|en|His feet are '''dangling''' in the water.}}
#* {{RQ:Butler Hudibras
|year=1905|passage=He'd rather on a gibbet '''dangle''' / Than miss his dear delight, to wrangle.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Enoch Arden
|passage=From her lifted hand / '''Dangled''' a length of ribbon.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang|ice hockey|lacrosse}} The action of performing a move or [[deke]] with the puck in order to get past a defender or goalie; perhaps because of the resemblance to dangling the puck on a string.
#: {{ux|en|He '''dangled''' around three players and the goalie to score.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[hang]] or [[trail]] something loosely.
#: {{ux|en|I like to sit on the edge and '''dangle''' my feet in the water.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative|by extension}} To put [[forth]] as a [[possibility]].
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=December 10 2020
|author=Ignatiy Vishnevetsky
|title=The first movie inspired by the pandemic is here, and it sucks
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=https://film.avclub.com/the-first-movie-inspired-by-the-pandemic-is-here-and-i-1845848429
|passage=That it happens to have been produced under the imprimatur of Michael Bay '''dangles''' the possibility of poor taste, but unfortunately, bombast and conspicuous consumption are nowhere to be found.
|accessdate=13 December 2020
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20201221205654/https://film.avclub.com/the-first-movie-inspired-by-the-pandemic-is-here-and-i-1845848429
|archivedate=21 December 2020
}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|dated}} To trail or follow around.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1833|title=Miller's Modern Acting Drama
|passage=To '''dangle''' at the elbow of a wench who can't make up her mind to accept the common title of wife, till she has been courted a certain number of weeks — so the old blinker, her father, says.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|intransitive}} Of a [[patient]]: to be positioned with the [[leg]]s hanging over the edge of the [[bed]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1976|author=R. Winifred Heyward Johnson; Douglass W. Johnson|title=Introduction to Nursing Care|page=139
|passage=Record the time and duration of '''dangling''', patient's pulse and respirations and patient's general tolerance of the procedure. {{...}} The next step usually in getting the patient out of bed is sitting {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Judith M. Wilkinson; Leslie S. Treas|title=Pocket Nursing Skills: What You Need to Know Now
|passage=[P]ivot to bring the patient's legs over the side of the bed. Be Smart! Stay with the patient as he '''dangles'''.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|transitive}} To position (a patient) in this way.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Judith M. Wilkinson; Leslie S. Treas|title=Pocket Nursing Skills: What You Need to Know Now
|passage=Using proper body mechanics for '''dangling''' a patient at the side of the bed.}}

